    "content": "<p>Load this example and press Run. The corpus is a set of Finnish and Estonian cognates — one row per meaning, one column per lect, with whole unsegmented words. What comes back is a table of correspondence classes: which segment in one lect answers to which segment in the other, how often, and in which cognate sets.</p><p>That is what regulae does. It takes cognate data and returns the systematic sound correspondences in it. Everything else on this page — the environments, the statistics, the outlier ranking — is structure discovered inside that table.</p><p>The words are split into segments through merkmal, so multi-character graphemes stay whole: <code>pʰ</code>, <code>t͡ʃ</code>, <code>kʷ</code> and a vowel carrying a combining diacritic are each one segment. You do not need to segment anything yourself.</p>",
